@meta
id: 03-15
title: Wire the payment service back in
chapter: 03
type: lesson
skills: insert-I, insert-A, open-o, insert-ctrl-w
par: 230
minutes: 10
vscode: five clicks in four places and a lot of retyping
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
`OrderProvider` calls a payment service it never had injected, and the
method it calls was deleted when the service was rewritten. Five changes,
one file, top to bottom.

  1. A third import, under the other two:

         import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

  2. A second constructor parameter, under the first, comma and all:

         @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,

  3. The refund call is `refundLegacy`. It is now just `refund`, and the
     rest of the call is unchanged.

  4. That same call is not awaited, and has no semicolon.

  5. The `return order` line has no semicolon either.

Every one of those is a key from this chapter. None of them needs you to
move the cursor sideways, and none of them needs Enter. Plan the whole run
before you press anything: which door, in which order, and how many `j`
between them.

@keys
| `o` | open a line below |
| `I` | insert at the first non-blank character |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `Ctrl-W` | delete the word before the cursor |
| `Ctrl-U` | clear the line back to the indent |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
Top to bottom means every move is `j` and you never have to work out where
you are. Remember that each line you open pushes everything below it down
by one.
--
Both new lines are `o` on the line above them. The `await` is `I`. Both
semicolons are `A`. The method name is the only one with a choice in it.
--
For `refundLegacy`, get to the end of the line with `A` and walk back with
`Ctrl-W`. It stops at every dot and bracket, so it takes six presses to
get back to `this.paymentService.`, and then you type the rest of the call
and the semicolon in one go. `Ctrl-U` and retyping the whole line is the
other honest answer, and costs about twenty more keystrokes.

@solution
One run, top to bottom:

    j o import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service'; <Esc>
    j j j j j o @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService, <Esc>
    j j j j j j I await<Space> <Esc>
    A <Ctrl-W>×6 refund(order.id); <Esc>
    j j A ; <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Two hundred and two keystrokes, and a hundred and forty-four of them are
the two long lines you were asked to add. The other three edits cost
fifty-eight between them, the typing included.

That ratio is the whole point of the chapter, so it is worth saying
plainly. When you are fluent, the cost of an edit in vim is the cost of
typing the new text, and nothing else. No positioning, no selecting, no
deleting what you are about to replace. If your keystroke count is much
higher than the number of characters you added, you are paying for
navigation, and navigation is what every remaining chapter of this belt is
about making cheap.

Three habits to take into chapter 4:

- Work top to bottom when the edits allow it. It turns navigation into one
  repeated key and removes the chance of getting lost.
- Choose the door before you press anything. `I`, `A`, `o`, `O` — one of
  them is always closer to the edit than `i` plus steering.
- `Esc` the moment the words stop. The insert-mode keys in this chapter,
  `Ctrl-W`, `Ctrl-U`, `Ctrl-O` and `gi`, exist to make a burst of typing
  survivable, not to make insert mode somewhere to live.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    this.paymentService.refundLegacy(order.id)

    return order
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return order;
  }
}
